Subject: compose: stop checking whether a persona's fonts are provisioned

The compositor asked the disk whether each font pack's families had been copied
into the profile before it would offer the pack, and reported a core pack it
could not verify as a limitation. The check never worked and could not have
worked. It looked for each family as a path under the user data directory's
fonts subdirectory; nothing in the tree ever wrote that directory, no code read
the fonts.provisioned_directory field that documented it, and the host's own
installed families cannot be enumerated from base/ at composition time because
that is Skia's job and Skia starts later. So the answer was always no, and the
sentence it printed said the claimed platform was missing faces it cannot be
without, on a host that had every one of them. That text sent a reader of this
repository down the wrong path today, which is a good enough reason to delete it
on its own.

The policy it was built for has been replaced. The user installs the claimed
platform's fonts into the operating system's font directories and the browser
assumes they are there, with the setup step stated in the documentation rather
than probed at launch. That is what the reference product does, and it is the
right division: the binary cannot see the host's font set before the graphics
stack exists, and the user can see it immediately.

This is not the same rule as memory, and the difference is worth stating because
the two look contradictory. A drawn memory bucket is clamped down to the host's
installed RAM, because a user cannot install memory to make the claim true and an
unbacked claim is falsifiable by allocating. Fonts are assumed, because a user
can install them. The distinguishing factor is whether the user can change the
host, not how loud the tell is.

Assuming provisioning means the compositor stops checking, not that it starts
claiming faces that are absent. Enumeration is served from the composed
allowlist, and that filter is subtractive: a family whose files really are
missing still fails to match, exactly as it does on any other browser. Nothing
here can conjure a typeface.

What goes: the fonts subdirectory constant, FamiliesProvisioned and its
PathExists walk, the fonts_directory parameter threaded through FilterServable,
the local that computed it in Compose, and both limitation texts. The
Servability::kFilesPresent enumerator stays. The compiled tables no longer
select it -- the font_packs axis declares no servability now, so a pack is
decided by the seed's subset draw alone -- but the enumerator is part of the
table format the generator writes, and the switch over it is exhaustive.

FilterOutcome loses its limitations vector along with the loop that drained it,
because the files-present arm was the only thing that ever pushed to it. The
struct keeps its single options member; it is a named return value and a later
servability rule may want notes again.

Compile-unverified: this cannot be built in this session.

